WEBVTT

00:00:00.705 --> 00:00:04.172
A classifier looks at this photo and gives
three raw scores:

00:00:04.192 --> 00:00:06.222
cat, dog and background.

00:00:06.242 --> 00:00:08.335
How do scores become confidence?

00:00:08.355 --> 00:00:10.480
And when it's wrong, how does it learn?

00:00:10.635 --> 00:00:15.865
Here the scores are zero for cat, one for dog,
and minus one for background.

00:00:15.885 --> 00:00:20.460
Scores can be any number, even negative, so
they are not probabilities yet.

00:00:20.755 --> 00:00:22.672
Step one: exponentiate.

00:00:22.692 --> 00:00:24.660
Every bar turns positive.

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:28.235
Zero becomes one, one becomes about two point
seven two,

00:00:28.255 --> 00:00:31.235
and minus one becomes zero point three seven.

00:00:31.255 --> 00:00:33.330
Bigger scores grow faster.

00:00:33.635 --> 00:00:38.240
Step two: stack them and divide by the total,
about four point oh nine.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:40.652
Now they fill exactly one hundred percent:

00:00:40.672 --> 00:00:45.527
dog sixty-six point five, cat twenty-four
point five, background nine.

00:00:45.548 --> 00:00:47.285
Softmax says dog.

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:48.372
Quick check.

00:00:48.392 --> 00:00:50.310
Add one hundred to every score.

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:52.147
What happens to the probabilities?

00:00:52.168 --> 00:00:53.830
Pause and decide.

00:00:56.645 --> 00:00:57.988
Nothing changes.

00:00:58.008 --> 00:01:00.625
Every exponential gains the same factor,

00:01:00.645 --> 00:01:03.812
e to the hundred, and it cancels when we
divide.

00:01:03.832 --> 00:01:08.995
That's why code subtracts the largest score
first: same answer, no overflow.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:11.045
But the photo is a cat.

00:01:11.065 --> 00:01:16.008
The loss is minus the log of the cat
probability: about one point four one.

00:01:16.027 --> 00:01:19.708
Push that probability toward one, and the loss
melts away.

00:01:19.727 --> 00:01:22.440
Confident and wrong, and it explodes.

00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:26.700
To learn, the network needs the gradient of
the loss for each score.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:31.187
It is p minus y: the probabilities minus the
one-hot answer.

00:01:31.207 --> 00:01:36.295
Cat's entry is negative, the others positive,
and together they sum to zero.

00:01:36.645 --> 00:01:37.125
Why?

00:01:37.145 --> 00:01:41.450
The loss is minus the cat score plus the log
of the sum of exponentials.

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:45.250
Nudge any score, and that log rises by its
probability.

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:47.812
The cat score also brings a minus one.

00:01:47.832 --> 00:01:51.475
So stepping against the gradient pushes cat up
and the others down.

00:01:51.495 --> 00:01:53.670
One step, and cat leads.

00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:57.337
One more knob: divide the scores by a
temperature.

00:01:57.357 --> 00:01:59.788
Below one, the distribution sharpens.

00:01:59.807 --> 00:02:01.762
Above one, it flattens.

00:02:01.782 --> 00:02:05.570
Distillation and contrastive learning tune
exactly this.

00:02:06.085 --> 00:02:08.640
You'll find this in every classification head,

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:11.328
in the class scores of each detected box,

00:02:11.348 --> 00:02:15.260
and in segmentation, as a softmax at every
pixel.

00:02:15.795 --> 00:02:17.175
Remember three things.

00:02:17.195 --> 00:02:20.450
Softmax: exponentiate, then normalise.

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:23.825
The loss is minus the log of the true class
probability.

00:02:23.845 --> 00:02:26.695
And its gradient is simply p minus y.

